WEBVTT

00:06.260 --> 00:15.320
In this section, you'll learn about etherchannel, which can be used to bundle multiple physical interfaces

00:15.560 --> 00:18.960
into a single logical interface.

00:19.310 --> 00:24.460
I'll cover why we need etherchannel and spoiler alert,

00:24.470 --> 00:27.170
it's because of spanning-tree.

00:27.660 --> 00:34.990
I'll also cover how the etherchannel load-balancing and redundancy works.

00:35.390 --> 00:43.310
You'll learn what all the different protocols that are available for etherchannel, how to configure and

00:43.310 --> 00:45.200
how to verify them.

00:45.860 --> 00:53.510
And then finally, we'll talk about another problem where if your switches uplinking to two different

00:53.510 --> 01:03.170
switches for redundancy, even if you're using etherchannel, spanning-tree is going to cut your available

01:03.170 --> 01:04.310
bandwidth in half.

01:04.690 --> 01:05.150
Cisco do

01:05.150 --> 01:13.880
have some technologies that will avoid that though - StackWise, VSS and vPC.

01:13.880 --> 01:21.440
We will cover an overview of those multi-chassis etherchannel options in the last lecture.
